# Mutation cases for §scxml-B-2-8-1 on the one branch of `EventContextGuard`
# that only a NESTED delivery can reach.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/nested_delivery_restores_the_outer_event_ctest.cases`.
#
# `~EventContextGuard` restores the saved event under
# `if (actionExecutorImpl_ && isNested_)`, and `isNested_` is
# `!savedEvent_.name.empty()` — true only when a delivery began while another was
# already bound. Every other document in the integration suite delivers at top
# level, so that predicate is false throughout and the branch is dead code as far
# as the suite can see.
#
# Measured 2026-08-26: guarding the branch out with `if (false && ...)` left the
# whole integration suite green at 369 tests. A mutation nothing can catch is a
# case that reads as covered and proves nothing, which is why this casefile
# exists rather than a case in `undecodable_payload_is_reported_ctest` — that
# fixture declares itself single-axis and cannot produce nesting at all.
#
# The oracle is the document that can: an external event whose transition enters
# a compound state whose `<final>` carries `<donedata>`, so `done.state.<parent>`
# is queued INTERNALLY and drained inside the guard the outer event built. The
# engine says so itself —
#
#   EventContextGuard: Nested event processing - saving _event='outer',
#   setting new _event='done.state.wrapper'
#
# — and what the restore owes is checked after `processEvent` returns: `_event` is
# the outer event again, because the outer guard restores nothing of its own.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_ctest --test-dir build -R '^IntegrationTests$'
mutation_targets sce/src/runtime/StateMachine.cpp
mutation_oracles tests/integration/NestedDeliveryKeepsTheOuterPayloadReportTest.cpp

# ── The restore never runs ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Guarded out rather than deleted, deliberately: deleting the body would leave
# `savedEvent_` unread and this file compiles with warnings as errors, which
# would report INCONCLUSIVE — a mutation the compiler refused — instead of a
# verdict about the tests.

mutation_case "the guard never puts the outer event back" <<'PY'
edit("sce/src/runtime/StateMachine.cpp",
     """            if (actionExecutorImpl_ && isNested_) {
                // Restore saved event
                actionExecutorImpl_->setCurrentEvent(savedEvent_);""",
     """            if (false && actionExecutorImpl_ && isNested_) {
                // Restore saved event
                actionExecutorImpl_->setCurrentEvent(savedEvent_);""")
PY

# ── Why there is no second case ────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ A case widening the branch to `if (actionExecutorImpl_)` — restore
# unconditionally — was written, run, and REMOVED. It came back SURVIVED twice,
# and the second time with an assertion added specifically to catch it, which is
# what prompted looking at why rather than trying harder.
#
# It cannot be caught, because it is a no-op in effect. A top-level guard's
# `savedEvent_` is empty, and `ActionExecutorImpl::setCurrentEvent` skips the
# datamodel update for an empty name outright:
#
#   ActionExecutorImpl.cpp:455
#     if (currentEventName_.empty()) {
#         SCE_LOG_DEBUG("Skipping _event update - no current event in context");
#         return true;
#     }
#
# So restoring an empty saved event changes nothing a document or a host can
# observe: the `isNested_` conjunct is redundant for `_event` and earns its place
# by saying what the branch means, not by changing what it does. A case whose
# mutation is behaviourally identical to the original reports SURVIVED forever
# and reads as a coverage gap that no test could ever close — this repository's
# discriminator ②, a case that is a no-op wearing a verdict's clothes.
#
# `ATopLevelDeliveryIsNotRestoredOverWithNothing` in the oracle still asserts the
# top-level half holds. It is a regression guard, not a witness for this
# mutation, and its comment says so.
